# securaCV desktop apps (the Flasher and the Lab) — Canary serial access +
# ModemManager opt-out (Linux). Both apps' .deb install this exact file, under
# two names (61-securacv-canary.rules from the Flasher, 61-securacv-lab.rules
# from the Lab) because dpkg refuses to let two packages own one path; the
# rules are idempotent, so having both installed changes nothing.
# canary-local/tests/desktop_parity.test.js holds the two copies byte-equal.
#
# Two Linux-only problems this rule removes for every Canary flow (detect,
# flash, live boot receipt, Vision module burn). macOS has neither, which is
# why the identical app "just works" there:
#
# 1) Permissions: opening /dev/ttyACM* needs membership in `dialout` unless a
#    udev rule grants access. TAG+="uaccess" grants the logged-in user access
#    on systemd systems; MODE=0666 is the universal fallback (same pattern as
#    60-rpiboot.rules).
# 2) ModemManager: a just-plugged board — or one re-enumerating after the
#    post-flash reset — looks like a modem. ModemManager opens the port,
#    probes it with AT commands and toggles its control lines, which the
#    ESP32-S3's USB-Serial/JTAG can take as a reset request. Symptom: the
#    write lands, then the port stays busy for ~30 s and/or the board is
#    knocked over mid-boot, so the live boot receipt never arrives. The
#    ID_MM_*_IGNORE marks tell ModemManager to leave these devices alone.
#
# 303a       = Espressif native USB-Serial/JTAG (every Canary's own USB-C port)
# 1a86:55d3  = WCH CH343 (the Grove Vision AI V2 camera module's port)
ATTRS{idVendor}=="303a", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1", ENV{ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE}="1", MODE="0666", TAG+="uaccess"
ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="55d3", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1", ENV{ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE}="1", MODE="0666", TAG+="uaccess"
#
# The catalog boards with no native USB reach the computer through a
# USB-to-UART bridge chip instead, and /dev/ttyUSB* is root:dialout 0660 on a
# stock desktop: the ESP32-CAM (its programmer board), the ESP32-WROOM
# DevKit and the ESP32-C3-DevKitM-1. These bridges are generic parts that
# plenty of other gadgets use, so they get seat access only (uaccess: the
# logged-in user, not every account) — never MODE="0666" — plus the same
# ModemManager opt-out. Scoped by product id, not by vendor:
# 10c4:ea60  = Silicon Labs CP210x (CP2102 / CP2102N / CP2104)
# 1a86:7523  = WCH CH340
# 1a86:55d4  = WCH CH9102
ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1", ENV{ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE}="1", TAG+="uaccess"
ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1", ENV{ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE}="1", TAG+="uaccess"
ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="55d4", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1", ENV{ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE}="1", TAG+="uaccess"
